WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.616
Here's a portfolio dashboard, the way
its reader sees it over morning coffee.

00:00:03.656 --> 00:00:08.928
Five stocks. One total, $65,098,
up 21%. It looks finished.

00:00:09.321 --> 00:00:12.213
But every number on this screen
arrived through a few quiet hand-offs.

00:00:12.253 --> 00:00:15.344
Places where one part of the system
takes another part's word for it.

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:17.082
So let's spend four mornings
with this dashboard,

00:00:17.122 --> 00:00:20.004
and on each one, let one
of those hand-offs slip.

00:00:20.217 --> 00:00:23.515
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.537
This week I contributed to Mycroft,

00:00:25.557 --> 00:00:28.662
which is both a book and a working
repository for finance pipelines.

00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:31.497
It calls them recipes. Two of
them sit behind this dashboard.

00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:33.407
One fetches the prices and does the math.

00:00:33.447 --> 00:00:35.494
The other calls it and draws the screen.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.157
My pull request, now merged,
added checks to both,

00:00:38.177 --> 00:00:40.749
and rebuilt the math step
so it runs on test data.

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:43.567
Every price you'll see
is invented, for testing.

00:00:43.607 --> 00:00:44.482
Morning one.

00:00:44.975 --> 00:00:48.033
The quote for one stock comes
back with a price of zero.

00:00:48.073 --> 00:00:50.071
The workflow reads the price
with a JavaScript "or",

00:00:50.111 --> 00:00:52.713
and to JavaScript, zero counts as missing.

00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:55.495
So it quietly uses the
previous close, 590,

00:00:55.515 --> 00:00:58.578
and the dashboard looks perfectly
normal. The math is right.

00:00:58.618 --> 00:01:00.922
The reader just doesn't know
which price they're looking at.

00:01:00.962 --> 00:01:03.675
I kept that behaviour exactly,
because it's the workflow's choice,

00:01:03.695 --> 00:01:06.910
not mine, and the recipe now
notes it for the maintainers.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.515
What changed is the record.

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:11.476
For every stock, it now
says which price was used.

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:16.052
Morning two. A response comes back for
a symbol the portfolio doesn't hold.

00:01:16.092 --> 00:01:19.085
In the original, the calculation
looks up how many shares you own,

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:21.288
finds nothing, and stops with an error.

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:23.473
Now the check before it
catches the response first,

00:01:23.493 --> 00:01:25.302
and sets it aside with a reason.

00:01:25.342 --> 00:01:28.851
And the calculation refuses to run
on anything that check turned away.

00:01:28.891 --> 00:01:31.303
Nothing half finished reaches the screen.

00:01:31.741 --> 00:01:33.874
Morning three, and this one surprised me.

00:01:33.914 --> 00:01:36.381
The portfolio, which stocks,
how many shares, what you paid,

00:01:36.401 --> 00:01:38.893
is written out twice
inside the same workflow.

00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:42.207
One copy decides which prices to
fetch. The other one does the math.

00:01:42.247 --> 00:01:45.020
Edit the shares in the first,
and the math keeps using the second.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.387
The total doesn't move,
and nothing says why.

00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:49.341
So the first step now reads both copies,

00:01:49.361 --> 00:01:52.302
and stops if they disagree,
naming the difference.

00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:55.681
Morning four. The dashboard
doesn't hold any prices itself.

00:01:55.721 --> 00:01:58.537
It calls the price workflow,
and it finds it by name.

00:01:58.577 --> 00:02:01.206
The ID that would pin it
down is still a placeholder.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:04.326
So the dashboard's first step now
checks that the name it calls is

00:02:04.346 --> 00:02:07.522
the price workflow's real
name, and stops if it isn't.

00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:10.150
And then there was the
morning it was my own check.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.033
To make sure these checks
could actually fail,

00:02:12.053 --> 00:02:15.724
I broke my own calculation on purpose,
and ran the test that's meant to catch it.

00:02:15.764 --> 00:02:17.053
It passed. All green.

00:02:17.073 --> 00:02:19.000
But the broken version
had never run at all.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.629
My test was grading a file
left over from the good run.

00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:25.208
A check that can't fail is worse than
no check, because it looks like one.

00:02:25.248 --> 00:02:28.778
Now the test won't grade anything
unless the broken version really ran.

00:02:28.818 --> 00:02:30.382
And then it catches the break,

00:02:30.422 --> 00:02:32.090
down to a single cent.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.030
So here's the same
dashboard, on any morning.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.476
Nothing on the screen changed.

00:02:36.516 --> 00:02:38.004
What changed is behind it.

00:02:38.044 --> 00:02:40.110
Four hand-offs that now
speak up when they slip.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.253
And some that still run on
trust. Fetching live prices.

00:02:43.293 --> 00:02:46.092
Three more steps in one recipe,
and five in the other.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.897
Both recipes are still
drafts, and they say so.

00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:51.320
Pick one number you look at every morning.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.243
What is it quietly taking on trust?

00:02:53.263 --> 00:02:55.397
And if that trust slipped,
what would tell you?

00:02:55.437 --> 00:02:58.551
A good answer names the hand-off,
and the thing that would say so.

00:02:58.591 --> 00:03:00.885
"It's always been right" doesn't count.

00:03:01.116 --> 00:03:05.665
Four mornings. Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI, signing off.
